NAME
       audit2allow - turn SELinux denials into policy rules, or explain them (audit2why)

SYNOPSIS
       audit2allow [-a]
       audit2why [-a]
       ausearch -m AVC ... | audit2allow

DESCRIPTION
       When SELinux blocks something, the kernel writes an AVC denial to the
       audit log (/var/log/audit/audit.log). audit2allow reads such denials,
       from standard input or from the whole log with -a, and prints the
       allow rules that would permit them, grouped by the process's type.

       audit2why (another name for the same program) reads the same input but
       explains each denial instead: whether a boolean would allow it, or
       whether a rule is missing.

       Read the output as a diagnosis, not as the fix. Most denials on a
       well-run system mean a file has the wrong label (fix it with
       semanage fcontext and restorecon), a service uses a port it is not
       labelled for (semanage port), or a boolean is off (setsebool -P). A
       new allow rule is the last resort.

OPTIONS
       -a     Read every denial in the audit log instead of standard input.

EXAMPLES
       Explain the most recent denials:

           ausearch -m AVC -ts recent | audit2why

       Show the rules that every logged denial would need:

           audit2allow -a

SEE ALSO
       ausearch(8), sealert(8), semanage(8), restorecon(8), setsebool(8)
